notis:
The Riksdag said yes to the government's proposal for a new Value Added Tax Act, so-called VAT (moms). The Act involves an adaptation to the EU's VAT directive. The Act is also intended, among other things, to clarify what applies when non-profit associations sell services internally.
The Riksdag has previously, in a tillkännagivande (formal request), pointed out that there had been uncertainties about what applies when non-profit associations sell services internally. Today, non-profit associations, such as the sports movement, study associations, and registered religious communities, are exempt from the requirement to pay VAT. This can also apply when an independent group within an association sells a service internally. For example, this could involve a large organization, comprising several associations, that has a joint department selling IT support to the associations.
If the service could instead have been purchased on the market, however, it may be judged that the VAT exemption leads to a distortion of competition. In such cases, the VAT exemption may not be applied. The internal sales are then assessed as subject to VAT. In its tillkännagivande, the Riksdag called on the government to investigate how the issue could be resolved without causing a distortion of competition, and without increasing the risk of fraud with the VAT system.
The new Act involves an adaptation to the EU's VAT directive, and must therefore not be applied more narrowly than the directive. According to the case law of the Court of Justice of the EU, consideration must be given to the purpose of the VAT exemption, namely to support civil society. The exemption must not be interpreted so restrictively that it loses its effect. In some cases, the new Act may mean that the VAT exemption is applied differently.
The Riksdag welcomes the new Act, and notes that the government has thereby satisfied the tillkännagivande.&nbsp;&nbsp;
The new Act and the legislative amendments enter into force on 1 July 2023.&nbsp;
